<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	Two entitlements, both genuinely used by shipping code. Neither can be
	completed from this repo: an entitlement is only half of a capability, and
	the other half lives on App ID com.flockcorp.flock in the Apple Developer
	portal. Codemagic's `app-store-connect fetch-signing-files` step (run with
	its create flag) builds the provisioning profile FROM that App ID, so a
	capability that is off in the portal produces a profile without it and the
	archive fails with "provisioning profile does not match the entitlements
	file".

	Status re-derived from evidence 2026-08-20, not copied forward:

	  Sign in with Apple  -  ENABLED ON THE APP ID AND SHIPPING. This entry
	    used to read "NOT YET ENABLED ... the current build blocker", which
	    stopped being true no later than the 2026-08-17 submission. Two
	    independent proofs: codemagic.yaml builds the archive from a profile
	    that `app-store-connect fetch-signing-files` derives FROM the App ID,
	    so a capability that were still off would fail the archive with
	    "provisioning profile does not match the entitlements file", and the
	    build that is in App Store review got past that step. The five APPLE_*
	    variables are also set on Railway, which comes from the .p8 issued in
	    the same portal visit, so 5.1.1(v) revocation runs for real
	    (backend/services/appleAuth.js revokeAppleToken) and the promise the
	    privacy policy and the deletion page make is kept.
	    Do not "fix" a future build failure by deleting this key: the button is
	    shipping (components/auth/AppleSignInButton.js, rendered on native iOS)
	    and Guideline 4.8 requires it wherever Google sign-in is offered, so
	    removing the entitlement trades a build failure for a rejection.

	  aps-environment: production  -  CORRECT FOR THIS PIPELINE.
	    "production" is the right value because codemagic.yaml only ever
	    fetches an IOS_APP_STORE profile and ships to TestFlight; a build
	    signed for App Store distribution talks to the production APNs
	    gateway. It would be wrong only for a Development-signed build run
	    from Xcode, which this project does not produce. Push is used:
	    AppDelegate.swift forwards the APNs token to
	    @capacitor-firebase/messaging and backend/services/firebaseService.js
	    sends alert pushes through FCM. Push Notifications is ON for the App
	    ID by the same proof as the entry above: the profile is built from the
	    App ID and the archive that reached review carried this entitlement.
	    What is NOT settled here is whether a given build can actually deliver
	    a push, and that is a different gate: codemagic.yaml writes a
	    PLACEHOLDER GoogleService-Info.plist whenever GOOGLE_SERVICE_INFO_PLIST
	    is absent from the flock_web group, and a build made that way signs and
	    installs fine while push stays off at runtime. Check the build log line
	    for that step before concluding push is broken on device.

	  Associated Domains  -  ENABLED ON THE APP ID 2026-08-26, and the
	    entitlement below was added in the same sitting. This block used to say
	    the entitlement was deliberately absent because neither half existed.
	    Both halves exist now:

	      1. The capability is ticked on App ID com.flockcorp.flock in the
	         developer portal, verified by reloading the page and reading the
	         checkbox back. Apple warns on save that this INVALIDATES existing
	         provisioning profiles, which is why the next build must regenerate
	         one. codemagic.yaml runs `app-store-connect fetch-signing-files`
	         with its create flag, so it derives a fresh profile FROM the App ID
	         and picks the capability up on its own. If a build ever fails with
	         "provisioning profile does not match the entitlements file", that
	         step is where to look, and the fix is a fresh profile rather than
	         deleting this key.
	      2. https://www.flockcorp.com/.well-known/apple-app-site-association is
	         served by frontend/api/apple-app-site-association.js, which reads
	         APPLE_TEAM_ID from the Vercel environment and answers 503 rather
	         than serving a wrong Team ID. The build in App Store review was
	         signed before any of this and is unaffected.

	    The domains are the apex and www, both, because iOS does not follow a
	    redirect when it fetches the association file: whichever host the link
	    names has to answer it directly. Vercel serves both from the same
	    deployment.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>production</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:flockcorp.com</string>
		<string>applinks:www.flockcorp.com</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
